WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.492
This slide makes the difference between ordinary inequality and NULL-safe inequality very concrete.

00:00:06.492 --> 00:00:10.982
Suppose we want billing records whose payment status is not Paid.

00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:18.672
With ordinary not-equal, a known status such as Open compares cleanly with Paid and returns TRUE.

00:00:18.672 --> 00:00:21.282
But when payment status is NULL, the comparison is UNKNOWN.

00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:23.497
That row is filtered out by WHERE.

00:00:23.497 --> 00:00:28.337
With IS DISTINCT FROM, the question changes slightly.

00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:34.642
We are asking whether the stored value is meaningfully different from Paid, including its missingness.

00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:38.792
NULL is distinct from Paid, so the row remains.

00:00:38.792 --> 00:00:40.377
Neither behavior is always correct.

00:00:40.377 --> 00:00:45.877
The right one depends on what the business question means.

00:00:45.877 --> 00:00:52.762
If the report should show only known statuses that are not Paid, ordinary inequality may be appropriate.

00:00:52.762 --> 00:00:56.737
If the report should show everything that is not definitely Paid,

00:00:56.737 --> 00:01:02.277
including missing statuses that need investigation, IS DISTINCT FROM is better.

00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:07.262
This distinction appears in compliance reviews, financial reconciliation, and data-quality dashboards.

00:01:07.262 --> 00:01:14.012
Missing information often needs attention rather than silent exclusion.

00:01:14.012 --> 00:01:17.212
Run both queries and compare the row counts.

00:01:17.212 --> 00:01:20.032
Then explain the difference in plain language.

00:01:20.032 --> 00:01:26.522
If you can explain the policy represented by each version, you understand the purpose of NULL-safe comparison.
